# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Rust ----

# Build output, including the shared library the app loads at runtime. Never
# commit it: `just build` regenerates it, and a stale binary is the single most
# confusing failure this project has (see docs/troubleshooting.md).
#
# It is also large: `cargo build --release` alone leaves ~600 MB here, and cargokit
# adds a per-architecture target for every mobile build.
/rust/target/

# The lockfile is committed on purpose: this crate produces an application, not a
# library, so the exact dependency graph is part of the build. The negation below is
# a no-op with the rules in this file — nothing here ignores it — but it is what the
# upstream flutter_rust_bridge template adds, and it keeps a future `*.lock` rule, or
# a contributor's global gitignore, from silently dropping it.
!Cargo.lock

# ----------------------------------------------- secrets and signing -----

# `android/.gitignore` already covers `android/key.properties`, `**/*.keystore` and
# `**/*.jks`, which is where Flutter tells you to put them. These rules cover the
# same file types anywhere else in the tree, because a keystore dropped in the
# project root would otherwise be committed with the release signing keys.
*.jks
*.keystore

# ---------------------------------------------------------- web build -----

# Output of `flutter_rust_bridge_codegen build-web`, which the generator writes to
# `web/pkg/` (the loader's `webPrefix`) plus a compiled `web/main.dart.js`. Nothing
# in this checkout runs that step yet — see docs/troubleshooting.md — so these paths
# only appear once the web target is wired up. They are generated either way.
/web/pkg/
/web/main.dart.js

# ------------------------------------------------- FVM (optional) ---------

# A local Flutter SDK install, managed by `fvm`. The `.fvmrc` pin next to it IS
# committed, so everyone gets the same Flutter version.
.fvm/

# -------------------------------------- what is committed on purpose ------

# Several things here would normally be ignored in another kind of project. They are
# listed so that a future contributor does not "fix" them by adding a rule:
#
#   1. Generated bridge code — `rust/src/frb_generated.rs`,
#      `lib/src/rust/frb_generated*.dart` and `lib/src/rust/api/*.dart`. Produced by
#      `just gen` and checked in so a change to the Dart/Rust boundary shows up in
#      review, and so a fresh checkout compiles before anyone has installed the code
#      generator. Regenerate them; never edit them by hand.
#
#   2. Lockfiles — `pubspec.lock`, `rust/Cargo.lock`, `ios/Podfile.lock` and
#      `macos/Podfile.lock`. This is an application, so pinning the exact dependency
#      graph is the point. (`rust_builder/.gitignore` ignores the plugin's own
#      `pubspec.lock` and `.dart_tool/`, which is correct for a package.)
#
#   3. `.metadata` — read by `flutter` to migrate this project between Flutter
#      versions.
#
# Everything else that is ignored here was verified with `git check-ignore`: the
# platform shells carry their own `.gitignore` files for `Pods/`, `Flutter/ephemeral/`
# and `Generated.xcconfig`, and `android/.gitignore` covers the Gradle wrapper and
# signing config.
